    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Have you ever had experiences where you heard, saw, or believed things that others around you did not, even during times when your mood was relatively stable and not feeling so low?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_3",
      "patient_response": "Yeah... yeah, I have. Um, actually, a little while ago, there was this stretch of a few weeks where I wasn't really feeling that deep, heavy sadness. Like, I wasn't crying in the car or anything. But even then... I still knew the cameras at work were watching me. Like, specifically me. And the supervisors, too, they were monitoring everything I did. I had to be so careful and guarded all the time. Which is exhausting, you know? Because the cameras are those little black domes on the ceiling. There's one right above the supply closet where we keep the extra linens and the, um, the non-slip socks. Those socks are always yellow or gray. I wore a pair once when I had to get my appendix out when I was sixteen. The hospital food was terrible, mostly just this weird red Jell-O. I hate Jell-O. The texture is just... it's like eating a rubber band. My mom used to make it with fruit suspended inside it for potlucks. She had this special Tupperware mold for it. It was seafoam green... but yeah, even when I wasn't feeling so low, I still knew they were monitoring me at work.",
